# MCP Client Wiring — hal0

Connect external tools (Claude Code, OpenWebUI, etc.) to hal0's MCP servers.

## Available MCP Servers

| Server | URL | Transport | Description |
|--------|-----|-----------|-------------|
| hal0-admin | `http://127.0.0.1:8080/mcp/admin/mcp` | `http` (POST-only) | Slot/model management, system admin, hardware probes |
| hal0-memory | `http://127.0.0.1:8080/mcp/memory/mcp` | `http` (POST-only) | Hindsight vector/graph memory, agent identity cards |

**Important:** hal0 MCP servers only support plain HTTP POST transport. Do NOT use `streamable-http` or SSE — these will fail with HTTP 406 or 405.

## Authentication

Both mounts are **token-gated whenever hal0's network auth is armed** (`hal0 auth status` shows whether it's on for this box; off by default on a fresh loopback/LAN install).

| Mount | Tier | Satisfied by |
|-------|------|--------------|
| `/mcp/admin` | ADMIN | `HAL0_ADMIN_KEY` only |
| `/mcp/memory` | CLIENT | `HAL0_ADMIN_KEY` **or** `HAL0_CLIENT_KEY` |

Send the key as a bearer header on every request:

```
Authorization: Bearer <key>
```

Both keys live in `/etc/hal0/api.env` on this host (owner-only, `0600`) — read them out-of-band; `hal0 auth status` reports only whether a key is configured, never its value.

**`X-hal0-Agent` is identity, not a credential.** It stamps the audit trail and routes the `private:<agent>` memory namespace — it carries no auth weight and does not substitute for `Authorization`. When auth is on, a request with a valid `X-hal0-Agent` but no bearer still gets a bare `401`.

### Rotating a key

```bash
hal0 auth rotate admin    # or: hal0 auth rotate client
```

A rotation is **not** live-propagated into an already-rendered client config. hal0's own bundled Hermes picks up the new key on the next `hal0 agent bootstrap hermes --repair`; any other client (this file's examples included) needs its saved `Authorization` header updated by hand.

